    imageQuery: "children lifejackets sailing lesson instructor supervising",
    body: `If your centre teaches anyone under eighteen, or adults who may be at risk because of disability, illness or circumstance, safeguarding is not an optional policy. It is a condition of RYA recognition, a legal and moral duty, and the thing parents care about most when they hand their child to you on a slipway. Good safeguarding is mostly not dramatic. It is a set of ordinary habits, clearly written down and consistently followed, that make it hard for harm to happen and easy for concerns to be raised. This article covers the essentials for clubs and centres in the UK and Ireland.

## The foundations you must have

Every centre working with children or adults at risk needs, at minimum:

- A written safeguarding and child protection policy, adopted by the committee or owner and reviewed annually.
- A named Welfare Officer (sometimes called a Club Welfare Officer or Designated Safeguarding Lead) who has done the appropriate training and is known to staff, volunteers, young people and parents.
- A code of conduct for staff and volunteers, and ideally one for young sailors and parents too.
- Safe recruitment: appropriate criminal records checks for roles that involve regulated activity with children, references, and an interview that asks about working with young people.
- Safeguarding training for everyone who works with young people, refreshed on a regular cycle.
- A clear procedure for raising, recording and referring concerns, including who to contact outside the club.
- Procedures for the practical risk points: changing, transport, overnight stays, photography and social media, one-to-one situations, communications with young people.

The RYA publishes model policies, guidance and training for clubs and recognised centres, and the national sports safeguarding bodies in England, Scotland, Wales, Northern Ireland and Ireland each provide frameworks. Use them rather than starting from a blank page, but adapt them to your site and your activities.

## Criminal records checks

The system differs across the jurisdictions: DBS in England and Wales, PVG in Scotland, AccessNI in Northern Ireland, and Garda vetting in Ireland. Each has its own definition of which roles require a check and at what level, and getting it wrong in either direction causes problems. Work out which of your roles involve regulated activity with children, apply for the right check through the right route, record the certificate number and date rather than keeping copies, and set a renewal cycle. Remember that a check is a snapshot, not a guarantee; it is one part of safe recruitment, not the whole of it.

## The Welfare Officer role

The Welfare Officer is the person a worried instructor, parent or child goes to. They need to be approachable, discreet, trained and genuinely available in season. They should not be the Principal or Chief Instructor if at all possible, because concerns sometimes involve senior people, and the Welfare Officer needs to be able to act independently. Give them a direct line to the committee or owner, the contact details of the RYA safeguarding team and the local authority's safeguarding contact, and the authority to act without waiting for a meeting.

## Make the procedures practical

Policies are only as good as the habits they create. Think through your actual day:

- **Changing**: separate arrangements for adults and children where possible, no adults changing alongside children, no phones in changing areas.
- **Supervision**: avoid one-to-one situations out of sight of others; where they are unavoidable, make sure another adult knows.
- **Collection**: know who is collecting each child, what happens if they are late, and never leave a child alone with a single adult at the end of the day.
- **Communications**: instructors do not message young people privately on personal accounts; use group channels that include a parent or go through the office.
- **Photography**: consent collected at booking, a clear position on parents' photos, and no identification of children by full name online.
- **Transport and overnight**: written procedures, parental consent, appropriate adult ratios and sleeping arrangements for any residential.
- **Medical and additional needs**: information collected before the course, held securely, shared with instructors on a need-to-know basis.

## Recognising and responding to concerns

Train everyone to notice the signs that a child may be at risk, to listen without leading, to reassure without promising secrecy, to record what was said in the child's words as soon as possible, and to pass it to the Welfare Officer immediately. The Welfare Officer then decides, with advice from the RYA safeguarding team or the local authority, whether to refer. Nobody at the club investigates; that is for the statutory agencies. Keep records securely, separately from general files, and for the period the guidance recommends.

Concerns about an adult's behaviour toward young people, including a member of staff, follow the same route. Have a clear policy on allegations against staff and volunteers, including when a person is stood down from contact pending the outcome.

## Adults at risk

Safeguarding is not only about children. Adults with learning disabilities, mental health conditions, dementia or physical dependency may be at risk of harm or exploitation, and Sailability sessions and inclusive programmes bring them to your centre. The same principles apply: safe recruitment, training, clear procedures, a named person, and referral to the adult safeguarding route in your area when needed.

## Culture, not paperwork

The clubs where safeguarding works are the ones where it is normal to talk about it. The Welfare Officer gives a short talk at the start of every season. The code of conduct is on the wall and in the induction pack. Instructors see senior people following the rules on changing rooms and phones. Young people know who to talk to and have seen that adults take them seriously. Parents are briefed and are welcome to ask questions. Reviews happen every year and after any incident.

## Data protection meets safeguarding

Safeguarding records are some of the most sensitive data you hold. Keep them in a locked file or a restricted system, limit who can see them, and retain them for the period the guidance requires rather than deleting them with ordinary records. Where you hold medical and consent information about children, treat it with the same care.

## Your annual safeguarding check

- Policy reviewed and re-adopted, with the date recorded.
- Welfare Officer in post, trained and introduced to the season's staff.
- Every person in a role requiring a check has a current one recorded.
- Every instructor and volunteer has done safeguarding training within the cycle.
- Codes of conduct signed as part of induction.
- Procedures for changing, collection, communications and photography briefed and visible.
- Concern reporting route tested: does a new instructor know what to do?
- Records stored securely with access limited.

Safeguarding done this way protects young people first, and protects your instructors, your volunteers and your club as well. It is one of the clearest signals a centre can send that it is run properly.`,
